00:00:05.213 --> 00:00:06.630
RYAN MATSUMOTO:
Cloud Shell Editor

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:08.490
makes it easy for you
to write code directly

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:11.310
in your web browser and
deploy it to the cloud.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:14.070
Powered by the Eclipse
Theia IDE Platform,

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.362
Cloud Shell Editor
provides a rich experience

00:00:16.362 --> 00:00:18.570
that makes it easy for you
to debug your applications

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:20.640
before deploying to the cloud.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.620
In this video, we'll
walk through an example

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:25.560
of how to run and debug a
local deployment of a Cloud Run

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:26.520
service.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.200
Cloud Run makes
it easy to deploy

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.080
highly scalable containerized
applications on a fully

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.940
managed server-less platform.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.680
But before you
deploy to Cloud Run,

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.180
you may want to run
your app locally

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:39.000
to simplify the
debugging process.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.210
First, visit
ide.cloud.google.com

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:46.110
to launch the
Cloud Shell Editor.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:48.310
On the status bar at the
bottom of your screen,

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:50.700
click Cloud Code.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.250
From the Cloud Code menu,
click New Application,

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.320
and then select the
Cloud Run Application

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.355
for the type of sample
app we want to create.

00:00:58.355 --> 00:01:00.480
After that, we'll get a
list of Cloud Run templates

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:01.870
to choose from.

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:04.690
Let's go with the
Node.js option.

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:07.290
Next, select a folder and
your Cloud Shell environment

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.840
to save your application
to, and give it a name.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.840
If that looks good, click
Create New Application.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:14.580
Cloud Shell Editor
clones the files

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:18.060
for your sample application from
GitHub into a new workspace.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.180
You can see the list of files
in the Explorer panel located

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.920
on the left side of the screen.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:27.030
Now let's go ahead and run
this application locally.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:32.930
From the Cloud Code menu, click
Run on Cloud Run Emulator.

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:35.847
In the run debug on Cloud
Run Emulator dialogue,

00:01:35.847 --> 00:01:38.180
you can choose your Builder
and adjust Build and Service

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:38.680
settings.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.810
We'll leave everything as
their default values for now.

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:48.800
After defining your preferred
settings, click Run.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.365
Right now, Cloud
Code is spinning up

00:01:50.365 --> 00:01:52.490
an environment that emulates
Cloud Run's production

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:53.570
environment.

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:55.670
You can monitor the
progress of your application

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:56.930
in the output window.

00:01:56.930 --> 00:01:59.030
You may need to click
Authorize to authorize

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:03.160
Cloud Shell to make Google
Cloud Platform API calls.

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:04.960
Once your app is built,
you can launch it

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.780
by hovering over the URL link
displayed in the output panel

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:10.229
and clicking Open Web Preview.

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:11.370
Congratulations.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:13.890
If you see this page, that
means you've successfully

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:16.650
run your Cloud Run app locally.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:18.385
Once your service
is running, there

00:02:18.385 --> 00:02:20.760
are some other options you
can access from the Cloud Code

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:23.370
Status Bar.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:26.730
You can open deployment
logs, open the service URL,

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:29.370
or turn watch mode on or off.

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:31.530
With watch mode,
Cloud Code continually

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:34.620
watch watches the file system
for changes to your files.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.750
If changes are detected, it
will redeploy your application

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:40.342
so that your edits are
reflected in near real time.

00:02:40.342 --> 00:02:42.300
Once you're done, you
can click the Stop button

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.300
to stop running your service.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.240
Now that you've learned how to
develop locally with Cloud Run,

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.960
let's take a look at how you can
debug a Cloud Run application.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.030
Click the Explorer
icon in the upper left

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:56.130
to display all the code files.

00:02:56.130 --> 00:03:00.450
Let's double-click on
index.js to open it.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:03.330
Lets go ahead and add some code
to the app's root directory.

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:05.730
We'll create two variables
and give them initial values.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:12.270
A third variable will be set to
equal to sum of the first two

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:15.200
variables.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:18.670
Finally, we'll increment
the first variable.

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:21.100
To add a break point, just
click in the editor margin

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:24.310
of the file you're debugging.

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:27.550
Once it's added, the break point
will appear as a red circle.

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:29.530
We'll go ahead and
add 5 break points,

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:31.420
starting with our first
line of extra code.

00:03:34.153 --> 00:03:36.070
Now let's go ahead and
run our service locally

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:38.740
with the debugger
session attached.

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:44.440
From the Cloud Code menu, click
debug on Cloud Run Emulator.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:47.230
Next, press Enter to confirm
that the application you

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:52.430
would like to debug can be
found in the listed directory.

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:54.440
Once you see the orange
bar at the bottom,

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:56.180
you are now in debug mode.

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:58.580
Launch the app by hovering
over the URL link displayed

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:01.730
in the output panel and
clicking Open Web Preview.

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:04.250
Now go back to the Cloud
Shell Editor window.

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:06.800
You'll see that the application
has paused at the first break

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:10.850
point, which is now a red
dot outlined in yellow.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:13.760
In the debug sidebar, you can
manage the debugging session

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:16.550
with the debugging
action icons--

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:23.810
continue, step over, step into,
step out, restart, and stop.

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:26.180
The debug sidebar also
include sections for you

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:29.750
to view information about the
call stack, variable values,

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:32.030
and break points.

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:34.260
If we open up the
local variable section,

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:37.930
we see that our three variables
start out as undefined.

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:40.410
Let's go ahead and
click the Continue icon.

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:42.810
This advances us to
the next break point.

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:45.300
We see in the variable
section that the value of foo

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:48.630
has changed to 5.

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:50.890
If we keep clicking
Continue, the values

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:52.840
of the other variables
change as well.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:58.380
We can also hover over
the variables in the code

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.240
to see what their
updated values are.

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:02.280
In this case, the
foo variable now

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:04.920
equals 6 because
it was incremented.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:06.730
Once we get to the
last break point,

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:08.640
we can click Continue
to run through the rest

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:09.870
of the program.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:11.610
Since there aren't
any errors, this

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:14.820
results in a
functional web page.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:16.860
Once you're done, you
can click the Stop button

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:20.250
to stop your debugging session.

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:21.700
And there you have it.

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:24.120
Cloud Shell Editor provides
a full IDE experience

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:26.190
that makes it easy for
you to develop and debug

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:29.280
all kinds of applications
right from your web browser.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:31.110
To get started with
Cloud Shell Editor,

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:34.950
visit ide.cloud.google.com.

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:36.570
Make sure to subscribe
to the channel,

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:39.030
and stay tuned for more
Cloud Shell Editor videos.

00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:42.080
[MUSIC PLAYING]

